  <h2 id="myth">Why "career in psychology" is really six different jobs</h2>

  <p>
    Search "career in psychology India salary" and most results give you one number, as if every psychology graduate ends up in the same chair doing the same work. In reality, a psychology degree opens six genuinely different working lives: a clinical psychologist treating patients in a hospital, a school counsellor supporting students, an organizational psychologist redesigning how a company hires, a researcher chasing a PhD and a UGC-NET score, a UX researcher testing how people use an app, and a people-analytics specialist reading HR data to predict who might quit. These jobs share a foundation in understanding human behaviour. They do not share a salary, a training timeline, or a licensing rule.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Where the single-number version goes wrong</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>It usually quotes clinical or counselling salary data and applies it to the whole field, when organizational psychology and UX research often pay more, faster.</li>
      <li>It rarely explains that RCI registration applies to only one of the six tracks, leaving people to assume the whole field needs a 7-8 year licensing process.</li>
      <li>It skips the non-clinical tracks almost entirely, even though HR, analytics, and UX research are actively hiring psychology graduates right now.</li>
      <li>It treats "scope" as a single yes-or-no question, instead of six separate answers that depend on which track you actually choose.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      A useful answer separates the six tracks first, then gives real numbers and real rules for each one - not one blended figure that hides more than it reveals.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="rci">The RCI rule that decides what you can legally call yourself</h2>

  <p>
    The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) is the regulatory body that governs who can legally practise as a Clinical Psychologist in India. This is the single most important fact in this entire guide, because it is also the most commonly misunderstood one: RCI registration is mandatory only for people diagnosing, treating, or delivering clinical therapy as a Clinical Psychologist. It does not govern counsellors, HR professionals, researchers, UX researchers, or people-analytics specialists, even though all of them may hold a psychology degree.
  </p>

  <p>
    To become RCI-registered, you need a UGC-recognised 4-year BA/BSc in Psychology with at least 55% marks (50% for SC/ST/OBC candidates), followed by the RCI-approved 2-year MA in Clinical Psychology. This course is genuinely changing right now: it is replacing the long-standing M.Phil in Clinical Psychology, with the transition tentatively rolling out from 2026, and includes supervised clinical training, assessments, and therapy practice. Only after completing this RCI-recognised course and registering with the Council can you legally call yourself a registered Clinical Psychologist and practise clinically in hospitals or private clinics.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Seats on RCI-approved MA Clinical Psychology courses are genuinely scarce - most universities currently offer around 10 seats per batch. That scarcity is a real filter, not a scare tactic: if you are set on the clinical track, plan for serious competition at the admission stage, years before you even reach the registration step. If that specific bottleneck does not match your timeline, the other five tracks in this guide need no such gate at all.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="degree">The real degree path from BA to registration</h2>

  <p>
    Before comparing tracks, it helps to see the actual sequence of degrees and where each track branches off. Most of the confusion around "career in psychology India salary scope" comes from treating this as one straight line, when it is really one shared foundation followed by five different branches.
  </p>

