          {item.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span class="tag">{tag}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Salary figures reflect current Indian salary-tracker data for 2025-2026 and vary by source, city, company size, and methodology. Compare more than one source before making a decision based on a single figure.
  </p>

  <h2 id="track-1">Track 1: Clinical psychology</h2>

  <p>
    This is the Regulated Zone track - diagnosing, treating, and delivering therapy for mental health conditions, and the only track that legally requires RCI registration. Fresh MA-RCI graduates at private hospitals often start near Rs 15,000-25,000 a month, while RCI-registered psychologists at government institutes like NIMHANS or AIIMS report in-hand pay of Rs 80,000-95,000 a month at Pay Level 10. With experience, a licensed clinical psychologist can average around Rs 4.7 lakh a year early on, rising to Rs 8-15 lakh with experience and a private caseload, and top professionals in established metro practice report Rs 25 lakh a year or more, with typical session fees of Rs 1,500-4,000.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Clinical psychology carries the longest training timeline and the sharpest pay gap between a fresh graduate and an established practitioner of any track on this list. It is also the track with the clearest social need behind it - India's shortage of registered clinical psychologists is severe and well documented. If your interest genuinely leans clinical and your timeline can absorb 7-8 years of training, this remains one of the most needed, if not the fastest-paying, tracks in the field.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="track-2">Track 2: Counselling psychology</h2>

  <p>
    This track sits in the Recommended Zone - school counselling, NGO counselling, and wellness-role counselling, none of which carry an RCI mandate. School and NGO counsellors typically start near Rs 20,000-35,000 a month, and mid-career counsellors with a private caseload or corporate wellness contracts commonly report Rs 6-10 lakh a year, though top-tier private practice in metro cities can reach considerably higher figures depending on reputation and client base. The training timeline is meaningfully shorter than the clinical route - typically a 2-year MA in counselling psychology, without the additional RCI-regulated clinical course.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Even without a legal licensing requirement, employers screening for school counsellor or corporate wellness roles consistently list a genuine postgraduate psychology or counselling qualification as a real filter, not an optional extra.
  </p>

  <h2 id="track-3">Track 3: Organizational psychology and HR</h2>

  <p>
    This track sits fully in the Open Zone and is arguably the highest-paying non-clinical use of a psychology degree in India. Entry-level HR analyst or talent-acquisition roles pay roughly Rs 4-7 lakh a year, HR business partner and people-analytics-adjacent roles reach Rs 8-14 lakh at mid-career, and senior organizational psychologists inside large MNCs or consulting firms report Rs 18-40 lakh a year. This track exists at the intersection of psychology and business - using research methods, assessment design, and behaviour measurement to improve hiring, retention, leadership development, and workplace culture.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most people never test this track because "psychology" sounds like a therapy word by default. But an organizational psychologist inside a large company's HR function is doing genuinely psychology-grounded work - assessment design, survey methodology, behaviour prediction - while earning a corporate HR salary ladder, not a clinical one. This is the track worth testing seriously if your interest in psychology is more about how people behave in systems than about individual therapy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="track-4">Track 4: Research and academia</h2>

  <p>
    This track suits people drawn to the underlying science of psychology itself, not client-facing work. JRF stipends run near Rs 31,000-37,000 a month for the first two years, rising to Rs 35,000-42,000 a month after upgrading to Senior Research Fellowship. Assistant Professors in government or UGC-aided colleges start on a 7th Pay Commission scale, with in-hand pay commonly Rs 45,000-65,000 a month before allowances, rising with promotions and central-university postings over a career.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    UGC-NET qualifies you to teach at the college level; JRF additionally funds a PhD through a research fellowship. Neither of these requires RCI registration, since teaching and research are not regulated as clinical practice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="track-5">Track 5: UX research</h2>

  <p>
    This track applies psychology to technology and product design - understanding how real users think, decide, and struggle with a product, then feeding that understanding back into design decisions. No specific degree is legally required for UX research, but a psychology, human-computer interaction, or behavioural-science background is explicitly listed as a preferred qualification by many hiring teams. Early-career UX researchers with 1-4 years of experience report average total compensation near Rs 9.5-13.9 lakh a year, the broader average sits close to Rs 13.2 lakh, and senior researchers with 8+ years of experience report figures near Rs 20-22 lakh a year.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Entry into this track usually runs through a portfolio, not a licence: two or three real case studies showing your research question, method, and the product decision your findings changed. This makes UX research one of the fastest tracks to test and enter for someone with a psychology background who also wants to work in tech.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="track-6">Track 6: People analytics</h2>

  <p>
    This track blends a psychology grounding in behaviour and measurement with data skills - reading HR data to answer real business questions like why people leave, who is likely to be promoted, or which hiring channel actually produces strong employees. Most trackers put entry-level HR/people-analytics pay in the Rs 4.8-8 lakh a year range, though some large-company dedicated analytics functions report entry compensation closer to Rs 12 lakh a year - the gap depends heavily on whether the role is core HR with occasional data work, or a genuinely separate analytics function with its own reporting line.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    A degree in HR or analytics helps you get hired into this track, but psychology and research-methods training gives you a real edge in survey design and interpreting why the numbers move the way they do, not just reporting that they moved.
  </p>

  <h2 id="salary-table">Salary by track and experience, side by side</h2>

  <p>
    Bringing all six tracks into one table makes the real spread easier to compare - and shows why "career in psychology India salary scope" cannot be answered with a single number.
